{{- /* ~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf — the ARCH/OMARCHY path.

       tmux looks for a user config at ~/.tmux.conf OR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/
       tmux.conf, and on tmux 3.7c the XDG one wins when both exist (probed in a
       scratch $HOME: prefix C-Space from the XDG file beat prefix C-a from
       ~/.tmux.conf). Omarchy ships its own config at exactly that path, so
       writing ~/.tmux.conf here applied a file tmux never read -- the wizard's
       tmux-prefix answer had no effect, with no error and nothing in the diff.

       So on Omarchy the stack writes THIS path instead, and SOURCES Omarchy's
       config first rather than replacing it. That keeps its Alt+Enter splits,
       Alt+1..9 window switching, the Super+/ keybindings popup and its
       status-bar styling -- which `omarchy-theme-set-tmux` re-tints on every
       `omarchy theme set` -- and then applies the stack's prefix and behaviour
       on top. Later `set` wins in tmux, so order is the whole mechanism.

       The stack's own status-bar theme is deliberately NOT applied on Omarchy:
       its hexes are baked light/dark and would pin the bar to Catppuccin while
       every other surface followed the active Omarchy theme. Plain Arch has no
       Omarchy theme to follow, so it keeps the baked one. */ -}}
{{- $omarchy := eq (index . "distroId" | default "") "omarchy" -}}
{{ if $omarchy -}}
# Omarchy's own tmux config, first. `-q` so a missing file is not an error: this
# survives Omarchy being uninstalled from under us, and works on plain Arch.
source-file -q /usr/share/omarchy/config/tmux/tmux.conf
{{ end -}}
{{ template "tmux-core" . }}
{{ if not $omarchy }}{{ template "tmux-theme" . }}{{ end -}}
